# Question: how much time do flaky tests waste a year (failed runs × retries ×
# pipeline duration, plus checking each failure), and how likely is it to be
# more than 300 hours?
#
# Run it:
#   ubc --scenario engineering/flaky-tests
# Change an assumption, for example after quarantining the worst tests:
#   ubc --set 'flaky_rate = 0.5% to 2%' --scenario engineering/flaky-tests
# See which assumption matters most:
#   ubc --sens hours_lost --scenario engineering/flaky-tests
#
# Every "a to b" is a 90% confidence interval. The wait for a rerun counts
# whole: a red build blocks the merge, so the person who pushed has to come
# back to it. Multiply by the cost of an hour to get euros.
#
# Sources: John Micco, "Flaky Tests at Google and How We Mitigate Them",
# Google Testing Blog (2016), for how often runs report a flaky result; the
# other ranges are typical experience.

# Assumptions
builds_per_day = 30 to 80         # pipeline runs a day across the team; look at your CI
flaky_rate = 3% to 10%            # runs failing on a flaky test; Micco (2016): about 1.5% at Google; see your CI reruns
retries = 1 to 2                  # reruns until it goes green; typical experience
pipeline_minutes = 10 to 25       # how long a run takes; look at your CI
check_minutes = 5 to 20           # time spent making sure the failure is flaky, not real; typical experience

# Calculation
flaky_runs = builds_per_day * flaky_rate * working_days_per_year                           # runs a year that fail for no reason
hours_lost = flaky_runs * (check_minutes + retries * pipeline_minutes) / minutes_per_hour  # checking and waiting for the reruns

# Result
flaky_runs
hours_lost
P(hours_lost > 300)               # how likely do flaky tests waste more than 300 hours a year?

# Output (seed 1, 5000 samples):
#   flaky_runs = ≈ 585   90% [272 … 1260]   98% [190 … 1800]   ▄▆██▇▆▄▄▃▂▂▂▂▁▁▁
#   hours_lost = ≈ 327   90% [132 … 795]   98% [92 … 1090]   ▄▇██▆▅▄▃▃▂▂▂▂▁▁▁
#   P(hours_lost > 300) = 0.56
